Q1. @Component, @Service, @Repository, 	
Ans:
- These all are part of spring stereotype annotations.
- They mark classes as beans so that Spring can automatically detect and manage them via component scanning.
- They tell Spring “this class should be managed by the Spring container” and describe the role of the class in your application.
- They have slightly different role.

SpringBean
- A Spring Bean is an object that, Is created by Spring and its lifecycle is managed by Spring.
- Can be injected using @Autowired, constructor injection, etc.
All three annotations create Spring beans.


1. @Component
- Generic stereotype annotation
- Used for any Spring managed component.
- Base annotation for others like @Service, @Repository, @Controller
Use it when the class doesn't belong to service, repository or controller layers.

2. @Service
- Specialized version of @Component
- Indicate business logic layer.
Why use it instead of @Component?
Improves readability and structure
Semantically represent business logic
Can be extended with service specific behavior later.

3. @Repository
- Specialized @Component for data access layer.
- Used for DAO (Data Access Object) classes
Example:
 @Repository
 public class UserRepository {
	public String findUser() {
		return "DB User"
	}
 }

It has some special features:
- Automatically handles database exceptions
- Converts low-level exceptions into Spring's DataAccessException

All above are detected by component scanning and registered as beans.
