Q2. Difference Between @Component and @Bean in Spring
Ans:
Both @Component and @Bean create Spring-managed beans.

@Component — Automatic Bean Creation (Class‑level)
It is used on a class and Spring creates the object automatically using component scanning.
@Component
public class EmailService {
}

How it works
- Spring scans packages at startup
- Finds classes annotated with @Component
- Instantiates them and registers them as beans

@Bean: Manual Bean Creation (Method‑level)
Used on a method
You explicitly return an object to Spring
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public EmailService emailService() {
        return new EmailService();
    }
}

How it works
- Spring calls the method
- Takes the returned object
- Registers it as a bean

Feature @Component @Bean
type: Class‑level Method‑level
Bean detection: Component scanning Configuration method
Object creation: SpringDeveloper
Configuration flexibility: Low High
Third‑party classes: No Yes
Readability: High (cleaner) Lower (more config)

When to Use Which?
Use @Component when:
- You own the class
- Object creation is simple
- It belongs to your application logic

Use @Bean when:
- Class is from a library / third‑party
- Instantiation requires logic
- You need conditionals or parameters

You cannot annotate third‑party classes with @Component

Note:
@Component registers a bean via classpath scanning, 
while @Bean registers a bean via an explicit factory method, often used for third‑party or complex objects.

Can @Bean used within @Component??
Ans: 
@Bean can be used inside both @Configuration and @Component, but only @Configuration enables full mode, 
where Spring proxies the class to ensure singleton behavior for inter‑bean method calls. 
Therefore, @Bean should ideally be used inside @Configuration.
