Q3. @Primary vs @Qualifier
Ans:
In Spring Framework, @Primary and @Qualifier are both used to resolve ambiguity when multiple beans of the same type exist, 
but they work in different ways and at different levels.

If you have multiple beans of the same type and Spring tries to auto wire by type, Spring will throw:
NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

What is @Primary?
- It is applied on the bean definition
- Marks any one bean as the default choice when multiple eligible beans exist.
- When spring finds multiple beans of same type, it automatically injects the one marked @Primary.

Example:
public interface PaymentService {
    void pay();
}

@Component
@Primary
public class CreditCardPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Credit Card Payment");
    }
}

@Component
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment");
    }
}

@Autowired
private PaymentService paymentService;

Here,
- Spring injects CreditCardPaymentService automatically.
------------------------
What is @Qualifier?
- Applied at injection point and used to explicitly specify which bean to inject.
- Overrides @Primary if both are present.
- Best when the choice varies by context

@Autowired
@Qualifier("upiPaymentService")
private PaymentService paymentService;

- Spring injects UpiPaymentService, even though another bean is @Primary.

Differences:
@Primary is default bean selection where,
@Qualifier is Explicit bean selection.

@Primary: Usage location is on bean definition
@Qualifier: At injection point

@Primary: Priority is lower
@Qualifier: Higher. Overrides @Primary

@Primary: Flexibility is less
@Qualifier: More precise

Aspect @Primary @Qualifier
Applied at Bean definition Injection point
Scope Global default Local, explicit
Overrides ambiguity Yes Yes
Overrides @Primary❌ ✅
Best use case Common default implementation Context-specific choice
