Q5 Difference between field injection and constructor injection of @Autowired
Ans:
1. Field Injection
@Component
public class MyService {
	@Autowired
	private MyRepositiory myRepository
}

Pros:
- Very concise, minimal code

Cons: 
- Hard to test (no easy way to inject mocks)
- Breaks encapsulation (Field is directly accessed by spring)
- Cannot be final
- Not recommended in modern Spring Practices

2. Constructor injection
@Component
public class MyService {
	private MyRepositiory myRepository;
	
	@Autowired
	public MyService(MyRepository myRepository) {
		this.myRepository - myRepository;
	}
}

Pros:
- Recommended approach
- Supports immutability
- Easy to test(You can pass dependency manually) 
- Ensures required dependencies are provided

Cons:
Slightly more verbose

3. Setter Injection(at setter level)
@Component
public class MyService {
	private MyRepositiory myRepository;
	
	@Autowired
	public void setMyRepository(MyRepository myRepository) {
		this.myRepository = myRepository;
	}
}

Pros:
- Useful for optional dependencies
- Allows changing dependency later if needed

Cons
- Object can exist in partially initialized state
- Less safe that constructor injection

Notes:
Constructor Injection: Best practice 
Setter Injection: Use for optional dependencies
Field Injection: Avoid in production code.
